###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_04f2a2d5_e799_4692_a654_420e76f5acc1
:EMMO_04f2a2d5_e799_4692_a654_420e76f5acc1 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_4e9fd152_147b_4564_8999_089608f41aff ,
                                                           :EMMO_87ac88ff_8379_4f5a_8c7b_424a8fff1ee8 ,
                                                           :EMMO_fc9222ed_14af_419b_b9df_b6a8a04e9d38 ;
                                           rdfs:seeAlso isbn:9780471400653 ,
                                                        isbn:9780521408448 ;
                                           skos:altLabel "Gas"@en ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "GaseousState"@en ;
                                           :OWLRL2DLAxioms "Disjoint Union Of: SupercriticalGaseousState, Vapor"@en ;
                                           :conceptualisation """A gas is a state of matter in which particles (e.g., atoms or molecules) have enough kinetic energy to overcome the intermolecular forces binding them together, so they move freely and independently, filling whatever container holds them. Unlike a liquid, a gas has no definite volume and is readily compressible. 
A gaseous state can be categorised univocally whether the substance sits below or above critical temperature T𞁞​, because that alone determines whether a liquid–vapor phase boundary exists. Below T𞁞​ the gaseous phase is condensable by isothermal compression; at and above T𞁞 no amount of pressure produces a distinct liquid.
A gas differs from a plasma in that its particles remain electrically neutral and un-ionized; once a significant fraction of particles ionize, the system becomes a plasma, which responds to electromagnetic fields collectively."""@en ;
                                           :elucidation "A gaseous state is a compressible fluid, a state of matter that has no fixed shape and no fixed volume."@en ;
                                           :note "\"Vapor\" and \"gas\" are often used interchangeably in casual speech, but in the strict sense they are different branches of the tree, divided by T𞁞. A vapor can be liquefied by pressure alone; a gas cannot. This is why \"water vapor\" is correct (water's Tc​ = 647 K is far above ambient) while room-temperature N₂ or O₂ is properly a gas."@en ;
                                           :wikipediaReference <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gas> .

[ rdf:type owl:Axiom ;
   owl:annotatedSource :EMMO_04f2a2d5_e799_4692_a654_420e76f5acc1 ;
   owl:annotatedProperty skos:prefLabel ;
   owl:annotatedTarget "GaseousState"@en ;
   :etymology "The word gas was coined in the early 17th century by Flemish chemist Jan Baptist van Helmont. He derived the term from the Ancient Greek word χάος (\"chaos\": void, empty space)."@en
 ] .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_0b15f4ae_092e_4487_9100_3c44176c545c
:EMMO_0b15f4ae_092e_4487_9100_3c44176c545c rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_ec2c8ac8_98c5_4c74_b85b_ff8e8ca6655c ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "Dispersion"@en ;
                                           :OWLRL2DLAxioms "Equivalent To: Solution or Suspension or Colloid"@en ;
                                           :elucidation "A material in which distributed particles of one phase are dispersed in a different continuous phase."@en ;
                                           :wikipediaReference <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dispersion_(chemistry)> .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_0e030040_98a7_49b2_a871_dced1f3a6131
:EMMO_0e030040_98a7_49b2_a871_dced1f3a6131 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_ec2c8ac8_98c5_4c74_b85b_ff8e8ca6655c ,
                                                           [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                             owl:onProperty :EMMO_9380ab64_0363_4804_b13f_3a8a94119a76 ;
                                                             owl:someValuesFrom :EMMO_668fbd5b_6f1b_405c_9c6b_d6067bd0595a
                                                           ] ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "PhaseHeterogeneousMixture"@en ;
                                           :elucidation "A mixture in which more than one phases of matter cohexists."@en ;
                                           :note """Phase heterogeneous mixture may share the same state of matter.

For example, immiscibile liquid phases (e.g. oil and water) constitute a mixture whose phases are clearly separated but share the same state of matter."""@en ;
                                           :wikipediaReference <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mixture#Homogeneous_and_heterogeneous_mixtures> .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_0e6378df_1ce8_4321_b00c_ee9beea60a67
