###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_a2b006f2_bbfd_4dba_bcaa_3fca20cd6be1
:EMMO_a2b006f2_bbfd_4dba_bcaa_3fca20cd6be1 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_220b7201_d277_4dca_bf6a_5a5e2c4062dd ,
                                                           :EMMO_8b0923ab_b500_477b_9ce9_8b3a3e4dc4f2 ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "Solid"@en ;
                                           :elucidation "A continuum characterized by structural rigidity and resistance to changes of shape or volume, that retains its shape and density when not confined."@en .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_a6f34396_1b31_4f42_abc6_cce491336bf6
:EMMO_a6f34396_1b31_4f42_abc6_cce491336bf6 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_ce2b356f_ca4d_4d38_8a6e_0fdccc61a1b5 ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "IdealGas"@en ;
                                           :conceptualisation "A gas where the PV=nRT relation holds, with compressibility factor Z=1. Since no gas really fits whis model, an ideal gas is intended to be a substance for which this model is a reasonable apporoximation."@en ;
                                           :note "Strictly speaking, an ideal gas is a model, not a thermodynamic region."@en .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_a71e5554_b7c6_4d18_b032_096f0f55c116
:EMMO_a71e5554_b7c6_4d18_b032_096f0f55c116 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_4354ac74_7425_43ab_92e4_6dc19d1afee9 ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "Alcosol"@en ;
                                           :elucidation "A liquid sol when the dispersion medium is alcohol"@en .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_b9695e87_8261_412e_83cd_a86459426a28
:EMMO_b9695e87_8261_412e_83cd_a86459426a28 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_8b0923ab_b500_477b_9ce9_8b3a3e4dc4f2 ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "StateOfMatter"@en ;
                                           :OWLRL2DLAxioms "Disjoint Union Of: CondensedMatter, UncondensedMatter" ,
                                                           "Disjoint Union Of: Gas, Plasma, Liquid, Solid" ;
                                           :elucidation "A superclass made as the disjoint union of all the form under which matter can exist."@en ;
                                           :note "Besides the classical four state, IUPAC Goldbook introduces another categorisation dimension with the distinction between amorphous and crystalline state. A substance can then be the combination of these different categorisation schemes, such as liquid crystal or amorphous solid. However, between crystalline and amorphous states there may be intermediate state called semicrystalline materials where amorphous and crystalline phases are mixed."@en ,
                                                 "Besides the classical four states,we have also the categorisation widely used in material science between condensed (liquid and solid) and uncondensed (gas and plasma) matter."@en ,
                                                 "In physics, a state of matter is one of the distinct forms in which matter can exist. Four states of matter are observable in everyday life: solid, liquid, gas, and plasma."@en ;
                                           :wikipediaReference "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_of_matter"@en .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c457b6b9_5e73_4853_ae08_d776c12b8058
:EMMO_c457b6b9_5e73_4853_ae08_d776c12b8058 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_4a464c8d_8895_44a8_a628_aed13509f1bd ,
                                                           :EMMO_a2b006f2_bbfd_4dba_bcaa_3fca20cd6be1 ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "SolidGasSuspension"@en ;
                                           :elucidation "A coarse dispersion of gas in a solid continuum phase."@en .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c74d40bb_83a9_46a0_b248_6647e945ba7c
:EMMO_c74d40bb_83a9_46a0_b248_6647e945ba7c rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_f5dba4d2_41dd_4591_a9e2_d35f8aee99e8 ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "SupercriticalFluid"@en ;
                                           :conceptualisation "A gas with T>T𞁞 and p>p𞁞, single phase with liquid-like density and solvating power but gas-like diffusivity and zero surface tension. Continuously connected to both liquid and gas (you can circle the critical point without ever crossing a phase boundary)."@en .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_ce2b356f_ca4d_4d38_8a6e_0fdccc61a1b5
:EMMO_ce2b356f_ca4d_4d38_8a6e_0fdccc61a1b5 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_f5dba4d2_41dd_4591_a9e2_d35f8aee99e8 ;
                                           skos:altLabel "ProperGas"@en ,
                                                         "TrueGas"@en ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "Gas"@en ;
                                           :conceptualisation "A proper (or true) gas is defined when T and p fall between T>T𞁞 and p<p𞁞. This is the region of permanent gases."@en ;
                                           :note "The historical permanent gases (He, H₂, N₂, O₂) are simply those whose T𞁞lies below ambient, so they resisted liquefaction until cryogenic cooling below T𞁞."@en .


